module bindings.ruby.xml_dsig_document_rooted_selectors;

// An XPath selector that starts with `//` selects from the document root no
// matter which element it is evaluated against -- REXML documents this for
// `XPath.first(element, path)`, and the context element only constrains
// selectors that start with `./` or `.//`. A signature validator that resolves
// the parts of an XML signature with `//ds:Reference`, `//ds:DigestMethod`,
// `//ds:DigestValue`, `//ds:CanonicalizationMethod` or `//ds:Transforms/...`
// therefore takes those parts from wherever in the document they appear first,
// not from inside the signature it is checking, and `at_xpath` on the
// reference URI's ID takes the first element carrying that ID. Both are the
// signature-wrapping shape: a response holding a legitimately signed element
// beside an attacker-authored one that re-uses its ID passes the digest and
// signature checks while hashing the attacker's element. The remediation is
// documented in the XML Signature processing rules the selectors implement:
// resolve the Reference relative to the signature's SignedInfo, and reject a
// document where the referenced ID matches more than one element.
//
// The `ds:` prefix bound to the XML-DSig namespace is the technology anchor,
// and each selector is matched as a whole `literal:` token, so a selector
// rooted elsewhere (`//ds:Signature`, which locates the signature itself, or
// `//ds:Signature/ds:SignedInfo/ds:Reference` naming the full path) is not one
// of these tokens.
//
// The conjuncts take different routes, because the two token kinds confine
// differently in Ruby: a positive `literal:` conjunct on a function context
// falls back to the file-wide search, so a document-rooted selector anywhere
// in the file satisfies it, while `assign:` conjuncts are matched against the
// function's own facts and nothing else. The two assign conjuncts therefore
// carry the function's identity -- it resolves through `REXML::XPath` and it
// computes over what it selected (canonicalize, digest) -- and the file-wide
// literal says which file it happens in. A function that only canonicalizes
// or only selects is not reported, which is what keeps a signature-building
// method in the same file out: it constructs elements and never resolves
// through `REXML::XPath`. The tokens are AST-derived, so a comment naming a
// selector cannot stand in for the call.
binding xmlDsigDocumentRootedSelectors {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and node.context.literal in ["//ds:Reference", "//ds:DigestValue", "//ds:DigestMethod", "//ds:CanonicalizationMethod", "//ds:Transforms/ds:Transform"]
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, [".canonicalize(", ".digest("])
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["=REXML::XPath.first(", "=REXML::XPath.match("])
  emit issue code.SamlAssertionWrappingBypass at node
}
